; /dev/ion access for Android 17 platform policy.
;
; Delivered on the boot ramdisk and compiled into the policy by init at
; first-stage `init selinux_setup` -- see install_ion_policy() in
; anykernel.sh for the why and the caveats.
;
; The node on an A17 GSI boot:
;   label   u:object_r:device:s0    (android17-release private/file_contexts
;                                    dropped "/dev/ion u:object_r:ion_device:s0",
;                                    so the catch-all at line 96 claims it)
;   DAC     0666 system:graphics    (vendor ueventd.rc -- not the problem)
;
; AOSP grants no domain open/read/write on device:chr_file
; (private/domain.te:765, "Rather force a relabel to a more specific type"),
; so the allocator's open() fails EACCES, mali_gralloc cannot allocate, and
; SurfaceFlinger aborts at the OEM logo. Rules written against ion_device are
; inert while the label stays `device`, and init cannot relabel it
; (private/init.te:150 grants init chr_file relabelto only for console,
; null, ptmx, random, kmsg, dm, dm_user and loop_control_device).
;
; This slot works because init/selinux.cpp:326 treats
; /odm/etc/selinux/odm_sepolicy.cil as optional and appends it to the secilc
; command line, and that compile passes -N (--disable-neverallow), so an
; additive allow against device:chr_file loads despite domain.te:765. The
; kernel has no neverallow concept at all -- it binds only the platform build.
;
; Only symbols the platform policy itself declares are named, so the compile
; cannot fail on an unknown identifier:
;   hal_graphics_allocator  an attribute, public/attributes:
;                           hal_attribute(graphics_allocator), expanded at
;                           compile time into its members. The members are the
;                           implementations the vendor policy adds with
;                           hal_server_domain, so this follows whatever the
;                           vendor names its allocator instead of guessing
;                           (hal_graphics_allocator is NOT a type -- every
;                           "hal_graphics_allocator" in AOSP's .te files is a
;                           macro prefix producing _client/_server). An
;                           expanded attribute with no members compiles to
;                           nothing.
;   surfaceflinger          a type, public/surfaceflinger.te
;   device, ion_device      plat types, public/device.te
;
; hal_keymaster is deliberately NOT named: android17-release has no
; hal_attribute(keymaster) and no such domain in public/, and naming an
; undeclared symbol fails the compile, which is fatal (LOG(FATAL) "Unable to
; open SELinux policy"), where a missing file is skipped silently. Add it only
; after confirming the name in the device's own vendor policy.
;
; The ion_device pair is inert while the node is labelled `device`; it is here
; so the file keeps working if a policy ever labels the node ion_device.

(allow hal_graphics_allocator device (chr_file (getattr open read write ioctl map)))
(allow surfaceflinger device (chr_file (getattr open read write ioctl map)))
(allow hal_graphics_allocator ion_device (chr_file (getattr open read write ioctl map)))
(allow surfaceflinger ion_device (chr_file (getattr open read write ioctl map)))
